# 6. Duty Schedule

Create a dedicated page called "Duty Schedule".

This page should provide a clear overview of who is responsible for what and on which days.

### A. Weekly schedule

Display a weekly schedule from Monday to Sunday.

Use a clean table or calendar-style interface.

Suggested structure:

| Day | Staff Member | Assigned Responsibility | Time |
|---|---|---|---|
| Monday | Staff A | Network maintenance | As assigned |
| Tuesday | Staff B | Laboratory support | As assigned |
| Wednesday | Staff C | Server monitoring | As assigned |

These are illustrative entries only. Actual data must be provided by the administrator.

### B. Daily view

Allow users to select a particular day.

For example, selecting Wednesday should display all staff members and duties assigned for Wednesday.

### C. Staff-centric view

Allow users to select a staff member and see:

- All their assigned responsibilities.
- Their weekly schedule.
- Their primary duties.
- Their additional duties.

### D. Duty assignment rules

A staff member may:

- Have multiple duties on one day.
- Have the same duty on multiple days.
- Share a responsibility with another staff member.
- Have a primary and secondary responsibility.
- Have different duties on different days.

The database must support all these possibilities.

### E. Conflict detection

When the administrator assigns duties, the system should detect potential scheduling conflicts.

For example:

- A staff member assigned to two overlapping duties.
- A task with no primary responsible person.
- A task without backup coverage, if backup coverage is required by the administrator.

Display a warning, but do not prohibit legitimate assignments.

